      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "**Assertion frameworks** provide expressive, readable ways to verify test outcomes. While xUnit includes basic assertions, frameworks like FluentAssertions and Shouldly offer more power and readability."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "comparison-cards",
      "data": {
        "title": "Assertion Frameworks",
        "cards": [
          {
            "title": "xUnit Built-in",
            "description": "Standard assertions included with xUnit.",
            "pros": ["No extra dependencies", "Well-known API"],
            "cons": ["Less expressive syntax", "Limited custom assertions"],
            "tags": ["xunit", "built-in"]
          },
          {
            "title": "FluentAssertions",
            "description": "Fluent, BDD-style assertion library.",
            "pros": ["Highly readable", "Rich assertion library", "Great error messages"],
            "cons": ["Additional dependency", "Slight learning curve"],
            "tags": ["fluent", "bdd"]
          },
          {
            "title": "Shouldly",
            "description": "Assertion library with natural language syntax.",
            "pros": ["Very readable", "Excellent error messages", "Simple API"],
            "cons": ["Additional dependency", "Less mature than FluentAssertions"],
            "tags": ["shouldly", "natural-language"]
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "summary-box",
      "data": {
        "title": "Key Takeaways",
        "points": [
          "Choose assertions that read like sentences",
          "Good error messages speed up debugging",
          "Consistency matters more than the specific framework"
        ],
        "takeaway": "Pick an assertion style your team agrees on and use it consistently."
      }
